    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-347617",
      "knowledge_point_id": "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨7-3t,\\,2{t}^{2}⟩,$ what is $|{r}^{′}(1)|?$\n\n$|{r}^{′}(1)|=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)=7-3t,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-3,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=-3.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=2{t}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=4t,\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(1)=4(1)=4.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =⟨{x}^{′}(1),\\,{y}^{′}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{r}^{′}(1)| & =\\sqrt{{(-3)}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "5"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "5",
          "choices_complete": false,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "hard",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-347617",
        "kp_title": "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-349469",
      "knowledge_point_id": "072ad553-dd0b-58e0-b3e2-082c6d9866dd",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Derivative of a Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Given that $r(t)=⟨{e}^{2t}(t+1),\\,\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}⟩,$ what is ${r}^{′}(0)?$\n\n${r}^{′}(0)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "We differentiate each component separately.\n\n- For the $x$-component $x(t)={e}^{2t}(t+1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =(2{e}^{2t})(t+1)+{e}^{2t}(1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(t+1)+{e}^{2t},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{2\\cdot 0}(0+1)+{e}^{2\\cdot 0}=3.\n$$\n\n- For the $y$-component $y(t)=\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}},$ we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{9-{t}^{2}}},\n$$\n\nand therefore,\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{9-{0}^{2}}}=0.\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{x}^{′}(0),\\,{y}^{′}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
