      "math_academy_id": "q-34724",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b31f2bdc-c267-5fc7-bd0f-283777ee4fee",
      "knowledge_point": "The Derivative of a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Find the derivative of  $f(t)={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k$ at $t=\\pi .$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(\\pi )$ we substitute $t=\\pi $ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =({e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi )\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+({e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -{e}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0+{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-1))\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j+({e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-1)-{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0)\\,k \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\pi }\\,i+{e}^{\\pi }\\,j-{e}^{\\pi }\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
