      "math_academy_id": "q-130405",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b31f2bdc-c267-5fc7-bd0f-283777ee4fee",
      "knowledge_point": "The Derivative of a Three-Component Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Find the derivative of  $f(t)=t{e}^{t}\\,i-3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}}\\,k$ at $t=2.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find ${f}^{′}(t)$ by differentiating each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t{e}^{t})\\,i-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}})k \\\\  & =(t+1){e}^{t}\\,i-6t\\,j-\\frac{6}{{t}^{3}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, to compute ${f}^{′}(2)$ we substitute $t=2$ into the expression for ${f}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =(2+1){e}^{2}\\,i-6(2)\\,j-\\frac{6}{{2}^{3}}\\,k \\\\  & =3{e}^{2}\\,i-12\\,j-\\frac{3}{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$",
